# 🔥 2️⃣4️⃣ Time-Saving Relationship

For a fixed distance:

[
\boxed{Speed\propto\frac1{Time}}
]

Therefore:

> If speed increases, time decreases.

Example:

Speed increases from:

[
50\rightarrow60
]

Time changes inversely.

If original time = 6 hours:

[
New\ Time=6\times\frac{50}{60}
]

[
\boxed{5\ hours}
]

---

# 2️⃣5️⃣ Percentage Change in Speed

Suppose speed increases by 25%.

Original:

[
100
]

New:

[
125
]

For fixed distance:

[
Time\propto\frac1{Speed}
]

Therefore new time:

[
\frac{100}{125}=0.8
]

So time decreases by:

[
\boxed{20%}
]

🔥 Important:

> **25% increase in speed ≠ 25% decrease in time.**

---

# 2️⃣6️⃣ General Formula

If speed increases by (x%), percentage decrease in time is:

[
\boxed{
\frac{x}{100+x}\times100
}
]

For 25%:

[
\frac{25}{125}\times100
]

[
\boxed{20%}
]

---

# 2️⃣7️⃣ If Speed Decreases

If speed decreases by 20%:

New speed:

[
80%
]

of original.

Time becomes:

[
\frac{100}{80}=1.25
]

Therefore time increases by:

[
\boxed{25%}
]

---

# 🧠 2️⃣8️⃣ Golden Relationship

For fixed distance:

```text id="8jts0e"
Speed ↑
   ↓
Time ↓

Speed ↓
   ↓
Time ↑
```

And:

[
\boxed{S_1T_1=S_2T_2}
]

---

# 🧮 2️⃣9️⃣ GATE-Style Example

A person normally takes 60 minutes to travel a fixed distance.

If speed increases by 20%, find new time.

[
S_2=1.2S_1
]

Since:

[
S_1T_1=S_2T_2
]

[
T_2=\frac{T_1}{1.2}
]

[
=\frac{60}{1.2}
]

[
\boxed{50\ minutes}
]

